[[contract-enforcement]]
Contract enforcement and drift

A contract is a declared promise about code — an interface, a schema, a documented
parameter list — that lives alongside the implementation. Drift is what happens when
the code changes and the promise does not: a function gains an argument but its
documentation still describes the old signature, or a struct loses a field a design
note still references. Left unchecked, drift quietly turns documentation into a
liability.

Enforcement closes the loop in two steps. A validation pass checks that the code still
satisfies every declared contract and flags the mismatches it finds; a repair pass
then updates the documentation or the contract to match what the code actually does,
or surfaces the conflict for a human to resolve. Because the documentation lives in
the same graph as the code, a changed signature can be traced directly to the prose
that has gone stale.
